(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-08"] = {"8.1":{"s":"Transition (d-block) elements sit between the s and p blocks, and their properties change gradually across. Inner transition (f-block) elements, the lanthanoids and actinoids, sit at the bottom of the periodic table. This chapter covers both families and some important compounds."},"8.2":{"s":"Transition elements are in groups 3 to 12 and periods 4 to 7, forming the 3d, 4d, 5d and 6d series. A transition element has a partly filled d subshell in the atom or in a common ion. Zinc, cadmium and mercury are not regarded as transition elements, because they have a full d¹⁰ shell in the atom and in the +2 ion. Copper counts, because Cu(II) is 3d⁹."},"8.3":{"s":"The general configuration is (n−1)d¹⁻¹⁰ ns⁰⁻². The 3d series runs from Sc (Z = 21) to Zn (Z = 30). Chromium (3d⁵4s¹) and copper (3d¹⁰4s¹) are exceptions, because half-filled and full d subshells are extra stable.","w":["Chromium is 3d⁵4s¹ and copper 3d¹⁰4s¹, not 3d⁴4s² and 3d⁹4s².","When a transition metal forms an ion, the 4s electrons leave before the 3d electrons."]},"8.4":{"s":"Transition elements show many oxidation states, because the (n−1)d and ns electrons have similar energies and can both be lost. Manganese goes from +2 up to +7. The most states appear in the middle of the series. Higher states are found in oxides and fluorides.","w":["Scandium has only one unpaired electron and shows few oxidation states; manganese shows the most.","Manganese shows the widest range of oxidation states, +2 to +7."]},"8.5":{"s":"All transition elements are metals. Except zinc, cadmium and mercury, which are softer and more volatile (mercury is a liquid), they are hard, shiny, malleable, ductile and good conductors. Most have high melting and boiling points, because unpaired d electrons give strong metallic bonding. They form alloys easily."},"8.6":{"s":"Across the 3d series, atomic radius falls at first and then stays nearly the same. Ionisation enthalpy rises slowly. Many compounds are coloured (d–d transitions) and paramagnetic (unpaired electrons, with spin-only moment √(n(n+2)) BM). Transition metals form complexes and interstitial compounds, and act as catalysts.","f":[{"x":"mu = sqrt(n*(n + 2)) BM","n":"Spin-only magnetic moment in Bohr magnetons; n = number of unpaired electrons. One unpaired electron gives 1.73 BM.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 8.6, p.171"}],"w":["Count unpaired electrons first: Zn²⁺, Cu⁺ and Sc³⁺ have none, so they are diamagnetic and colourless.","The spin-only formula ignores orbital motion, so measured values differ a little."]},"8.7":{"s":"Two key compounds. Potassium permanganate, KMnO₄, is made from pyrolusite (MnO₂) by fusing with KOH and then oxidising the manganate. It is a strong oxidising agent, and its action changes with acidic, neutral or basic medium. Potassium dichromate, K₂Cr₂O₇, is made from chromite ore. It is an oxidising agent, and chromate and dichromate change into each other with pH.","w":["KMnO₄ is purple. In acidic solution it is reduced to almost colourless (very pale pink) Mn²⁺; in neutral solution it gives brown MnO₂.","Dichromate is orange and chromate yellow; they change into each other when you change the pH."]},"8.8":{"s":"Common features of d-block elements: variable oxidation states, coloured ions, paramagnetism, catalytic activity, complex formation, alloy formation and interstitial compounds. Most of these come from partly filled d orbitals."},"8.9":{"s":"Metallurgy is the commercial extraction of a metal from its ore. The book names pyrometallurgy, hydrometallurgy and electrometallurgy. The steps are concentration of the ore, roasting or calcination to the oxide, reduction to the metal, and refining. Iron is extracted from haematite in a blast furnace, with coke as the reducing agent. The book describes the extraction of iron in detail."},"8.10":{"s":"In f-block elements the electrons fill 4f orbitals (lanthanoids, after lanthanum) or 5f orbitals (actinoids, after actinium). They are shown in two rows below the main table and are called inner transition elements."},"8.11":{"s":"Lanthanoids are very alike in chemical properties, because the 4f electrons lie deep inside the atom; this makes them hard to separate, and +3 is their common oxidation state. Actinoids are less alike: they show more oxidation states than the lanthanoids. All f-block elements are metals."},"8.12":{"s":"Lanthanoids are soft, silvery metals. Their common oxidation state is +3, with some +2 and +4. Lanthanoid contraction: atomic and ionic size shrink steadily across the series, because 4f electrons shield the nucleus poorly. As a result, the 4d and 5d elements of the same group (Zr and Hf) are almost the same size.","w":["The lanthanoid contraction is why elements of the second and third transition series (such as Zr and Hf) are nearly the same size."]},"8.13":{"s":"Lanthanoid compounds are present in every household. Inside a colour TV tube or computer monitor, mixed lanthanoid compounds glow in three colours when electrons hit them; europium and yttrium oxide gives the red. Lanthanoids are also used in the Nd:YAG laser, in erbium-doped optical-fibre amplifiers, in hybrid cars, in superconductors and in permanent magnets."},"8.14":{"s":"Actinoids are the 5f series, from thorium to lawrencium (Z = 103). All of them are radioactive. Only thorium and uranium occur in useful amounts in nature; most of the others are made artificially and have short half-lives.","w":["All actinoids are radioactive; the early members (Th, U) are the ones found in nature."]},"8.15":{"s":"Actinoids are silvery, very reactive, radioactive metals. They show more oxidation states than lanthanoids (uranium up to +6), because 5f, 6d and 7s orbitals have similar energies. Actinoid contraction is larger than lanthanoid contraction."},"8.16":{"s":"Uranium is an actinoid that is in great demand because it is used in nuclear reactors. Thorium oxide with 1% cerium oxide was a major source of indoor light in incandescent gas mantles, and there is still a great demand for these lights for outdoor camping, since the oxides turn heat into intense light."},"8.17":{"s":"Elements beyond uranium (Z > 92) are called transuranium elements and are mostly made artificially in nuclear reactions. Elements 104 to 118 come after the actinoids and are called postactinoid (transactinide) elements. They are extremely unstable, are made only a few atoms at a time, and their names are approved by IUPAC."}};
